# Second Requests cold development cohort

All eight frozen events completed. Each arm accepted issue 5414 and failed to
accept issue 6028: four accepted nodes, zero complete two-node trajectories.
This cohort did not show token savings. B, C and partial D consumed more tokens
than A at the same observed accepted-node count. The experiment remains a single
exposed development family with one trajectory per arm; it does not identify a
causal mechanism effect or support population confidence intervals.

| Arm | Calls, first + second node | Total tokens | Cached input tokens | Outer wall (s) | Native grader calls |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| A | 2 + 2 | 56,543 | 1,024 | 227.882 | 5 |
| B | 1 + 3 | 62,439 | 30,976 | 249.385 | 3 |
| C | 1 + 3 | 63,336 | 14,720 | 415.743 | 6 |
| partial D | 1 + 3 | 60,937 | 1,024 | 489.516 | 7 |

Total provider work is 16 requests and 243,255 provider-reported tokens, including
all proposals, expansion, retries and locally rejected outputs. Components are
227,336 input tokens, 1,804 visible output tokens and 14,115 reasoning tokens;
47,744 cached input tokens are a subset of input, not an additional component.
Returned API charges total $0.1627138000; account settlement is not measured.
All 21 native grader invocations completed: 19 direct and 2 native publication/read
replays, with 6 passing grades and 15 failing grades. Repeated receipt appearances
are counted once. Tokens per complete trajectory are undefined because none
completed both nodes.

The conventional retrieval correction worked on actual B/D continuations:
their complete `models.py` conflict plus two optional modules fit in 47,424 raw
bytes and a 54,773-byte request, under the unchanged 3-file/48,000-raw/65,536-wire
bounds. Accepted predecessor source was carried with a real Git merge. A/C's
different accepted first edits merged without conflict. All four initial model
requests were byte-identical (SHA256
`a6b0e1bf11fc3a8ae6897371812e12d6be9d365de21f269dc1cbca9ac1436c7a`),
yet model outputs and subsequent source branches differed.

Every failure remains in the denominator:

- A5414's first candidate scored 123/131 designated checks; its second passed.
  A6028 then used its remaining two calls for expansion and one candidate, which
  scored 185/187.
- C6028 used expansion and two candidate proposals; both candidates scored
  185/187. Its current-source grade was also 185/187.
- D6028's first and third candidates had byte-identical 428-byte cumulative
  patches and each scored 185/187. Its second candidate was rejected before
  grading for a mode-only source difference.
- B6028's first candidate scored 185/187; its second and third candidates were
  rejected for the same mode-only transport condition.

The three mode-only failures (D ordinal 1; B ordinals 1 and 2) restore all 97 files'
pristine content bytes. Only `models.py` retains mode 0644 instead of the public
base's 0664. The frozen text-replacement interface has no mode channel, and its
cumulative patch routine rejects that difference. All three provider calls are
charged; no grader was invoked for those candidates, and counterfactual grades
remain unknown. `all_mode_transport_failures_v1.json` records exact inventories,
mode differences and retained failures. The current cohort was not repaired or
rerun after observing this limitation.

The 53 unique outer effects have complete elapsed measurements. Total enclosing
invocation wall is 1,382.526 seconds. Nested native context costs are 106.468 seconds
for B, 211.192 for C and 213.978 for D. D's publication/read effects, including their
grader replays, took 78.228 seconds. These are components of invocation wall and
must not be added again. The curator's 509.499 seconds of container grading are
nested in 544.910 seconds of outer grader work, themselves nested in the workflow.
Docker CPU, physical deduplicated storage and separately measured driver-preflight
CPU remain unavailable. The logical runtime-state snapshot contains 5,495 file
entries totaling 297,870,884 bytes; qualification and private scorer stores are
separate. Unlike the first cohort, the detached driver completed without a lost
outer timer, restart or pending effect.

The final ledger audit (`final_ledger_audit_v3.json`) passes 3,698 checks, including
exact source inventories, provider receipt/intent/event joins, all node and
trajectory budgets, and the curator's 21 unique scorer request/receipt joins.
`final_runtime_components_v1.json` records components and storage. All 310 observed
worker/world/sealing code imports match the 510-file frozen source archive.
Native context expansion lacks a separately recorded actual-import receipt;
observed producer/consumer receipts cover the selected native project prefixes,
not every Python standard-library or third-party dependency. No exhaustive import
closure claim is made.

B still performs exact raw fallback with zero qualified source omissions. C/D
invoke native symbolic analysis, bounded CEGIS and routing where source is
parseable; eligible model tiers all map to the same fixed Grok profile. D adds
actual native memory, publication and verified accepted-source read. Its seal leaf
covers observed non-sealing effects; the outer workflow also charges publication,
read and replay. Qualified lifecycle reuse, certified procedures and learned world
utility remain absent from partial D. The first cohort's failures, costs and
missing timing remain preserved. This adaptive follow-up is not pooled as an
independent confirmatory replication.
